> I think it's worth pointing out that the predicate is only evaluated for explicit names. In general I think a performance penalty on the order of the explicit names in the request is not something we should worry about too much.

Fair enough. I had concrete names as a result of wildcard expansion in mind. But now I think this code should only apply to concrete index names directly supplied by end users. In that case, it is reasonable to expect the number of concrete names is small enough to not be concerned. I think we can also have something like

```java
if (authorizedIndices != null && authorizedIndices.contains(name)) {
  return true;
} else {
  return predicate.test(name);
}
```
which will run two tests if `contains` returns `false`. But `contains` returning false is an edge case already, so running an extra `predicate.test` is not really an issue. It helps largely maintain the existing performance profile.

> I think the AuthorizedIndices class should not implement the Set interface anymore. I can implement this if you consent.

That'd be great. This was my biggest gripe. It also helps get rid of the unused method like `containsAll`.

> True, but I think this has been acknowledged https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/issues/90215#issuecomment-1273249327

I forgot about that. Thanks for reminding me. 

Btw, since we are aming to have a single implementation between core and security, have you considered how to unify them (different behaviours for `ignore_unavailable` on explicit names) when the time comes? I guess it can be parameterized? Or something else?

In summary, I am happy for you to proceed as discussed above. Thanks!

> > I think the AuthorizedIndices class should not implement the Set interface anymore. I can implement this if you consent.

> That'd be great. This was my biggest gripe. It also helps get rid of the unused method like containsAll.

I have updated the PR to that effect. It's now a big boy.

@ywangd I like the suggestion from:
> if (authorizedIndices != null && authorizedIndices.contains(name)) {
  return true;
} else {
  return predicate.test(name);
}

but I've not incorporated it for now. I feel like it's a bit too smart to squeeze it in this PR. But I'm open to reconsider if you disagree.

---------------

So I think this PR should be merged if (cc @jakelandis):
 * we want to fix https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/issues/90215 in the current architecture of a Security filter that rewrites requests scoped by the user's access level
 * we don't have a better alternative than the proposed solution that entails we continue to expand wildcards to existing indices, but explicit name authorization is indiferent to indices existing or not

